{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Deep Chill: Psychrophiles of a Siberian Permafrost Thaw Pond",
    "date": "2026-10-13",
    "lesson": "As Siberian permafrost thaws, ancient organic carbon frozen for millennia is suddenly exposed to liquid water for the first time in thousands of years. Cold-adapted (psychrophilic) bacteria in the thermokarst pond water must digest this old carbon before it refreezes each night or diffuses away. Their enzymes work slowly in near-0C water (Q10 physiology means metabolism crawls), but the payoff is huge: permafrost carbon is mostly ancient plant lipid and peat polysaccharide, so lipid- and carbohydrate-digesting enzymes matter most. The lesson: cold does not stop microbial life, it just forces a different pace — longer runs between tumbles, slower division, but genuinely new carbon entering the cycle for the first time since the Pleistocene.",
    "citation": "Mackelprang et al. 2011, Nature; Vonk et al. 2015, Biogeosciences - permafrost thaw carbon release",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Siberian thermokarst thaw ponds releasing Pleistocene-age permafrost carbon"
  },
